---
.title = "The Rooms protocol",
.description = "The Rooms application protocol over OCapN: its objects, how you obtain them, and the methods they answer.",
.date = .date("2026-09-12T00:00:00"),
.layout = "page.shtml",
.draft = false,
---

Rooms defines methods and values for rooms, membership, and messages over
[OCapN](https://github.com/ocapn/ocapn)'s CapTP. A client calls an object it
holds. It starts by fetching the public `directory` object, which hands back
rooms and other members' inboxes. Optional operator and diagnostic objects
are published separately under secret names.

If OCapN is new to you, [Why OCapN]($link.page('why')) explains references,
promises, and introductions from the ground up, and
[Concepts]($link.page('concepts')) covers locators and durable links.

# The objects

Each card is one object: how you come to hold it, the calls it answers, and
what it hands back. They form a chain from the one published name down to a
single line of text. Cool cards live on the server, warm cards are handed to
you to use or pass on, and the inbox is the one you make yourself.

```=html
<div class="objects">
<p class="objects-tier">Published by the server</p>
<div class="objects-row">
<article class="object" data-tone="cool">
<p class="object-name">directory</p>
<p class="object-from">Fetch it by name. The public entry point.</p>
<p class="object-calls"><code>open</code> <code>openPrivateLink</code> <code>openManaged</code> <code>enter</code> <code>list</code> <code>watch</code> <code>register</code> <code>introduce</code> <code>offerInbox</code> <code>link</code> <code>listPublications</code> <code>namedPublications</code> <code>watchPublications</code> <code>enterPublication</code> <code>linkPublication</code> <code>unwatchPublications</code></p>
<p class="object-back">Hands back a <b>room</b>, a <b>name</b>, another member's <b>inbox</b>, or a room link. The publication calls list the server's published rooms and links. <code>listPublications</code> answers one page at a time, with a cursor to pass back for the next page.</p>
</article>
<article class="object" data-tone="cool">
<p class="object-name">inspect, peering, listeners</p>
<p class="object-from">Each is a secret the server prints when started with <code>--diagnostics</code>, <code>--peering</code>, or <code>--admin-listeners</code>. Fetch it like the directory.</p>
<p class="object-calls"><code>snapshot</code> <code>publishDurableRoom</code> <code>list</code> <code>up</code> <code>down</code></p>
<p class="object-back">Hands back what the server holds right now, the live listeners as rows, or a lease that answers <code>ready</code> and <code>revoke</code>.</p>
</article>
<article class="object" data-tone="cool">
<p class="object-name">operator</p>
<p class="object-from">The server's <code>--operator</code> secret, pasted into the <b>Holdings</b> tab. It fills the admin pages under <code>/ops</code>.</p>
<p class="object-calls"><code>operatorSnapshot</code> <code>operatorRetireMatching</code> <code>watch</code> <code>unwatch</code> <code>changed</code> <code>giveSteward</code> <code>giveManager</code> <code>revokeSteward</code> <code>revokeRoomLink</code> <code>unregisterRoom</code> <code>setRoomPolicy</code> <code>revokeRoomAuthority</code> <code>revokeNick</code> <code>disconnectSession</code> <code>removeOperatorRoom</code> <code>releaseOperatorReference</code></p>
<p class="object-back">Answers for the server's rooms, names, live connections, and the references each holds, and hands back a room's <b>steward</b>.</p>
</article>
</div>
<p class="objects-tier">Handed back by the directory</p>
<div class="objects-row">
<article class="object" data-tone="cool">
<p class="object-name">room</p>
<p class="object-from"><code>open</code> or <code>enter</code>, or a room link.</p>
<p class="object-calls"><code>subscribe</code> <code>called</code> <code>private</code> <code>roomPolicy</code> <code>managedRoomIdentity</code> <code>link</code></p>
<p class="object-back">Hands back a <b>seat</b>, a <b>subject</b>, or a room link.</p>
</article>
<article class="object" data-tone="warm">
<p class="object-name">name</p>
<p class="object-from"><code>register</code>, with the name you want.</p>
<p class="object-calls"><code>voucher</code> <code>signalCheck</code></p>
<p class="object-back">Hands back proof that you hold this name, for a second connection, and answers a route's recheck of an exact registered name.</p>
</article>
<article class="object" data-tone="warm">
<p class="object-name">bootstrap</p>
<p class="object-from"><code>openManaged</code>'s second answer, good exactly once.</p>
<p class="object-calls"><code>roomAuthorities</code></p>
<p class="object-back">Hands back the room's durable entry and manager sturdyrefs, as strings.</p>
</article>
<article class="object" data-tone="warm">
<p class="object-name">manager</p>
<p class="object-from">The management link <code>openManaged</code> hands you.</p>
<p class="object-calls"><code>link</code> <code>giveSteward</code> <code>revokeSteward</code> <code>revokeRoomLink</code> <code>setRoomPolicy</code> <code>unregisterRoom</code> <code>managedRoomIdentity</code></p>
<p class="object-back">Hands back a reissued room link or the room's <b>steward</b>.</p>
</article>
</div>
<p class="objects-tier">Handed back by the manager</p>
<div class="objects-row">
<article class="object" data-tone="warm">
<p class="object-name">steward</p>
<p class="object-from"><code>giveSteward</code> on the manager, or the operator's admin page. It is created once per room, and asking again hands back the same one.</p>
<p class="object-calls"><code>kick</code> <code>governs</code></p>
<p class="object-back">Hands back nothing. A member is asked to leave.</p>
</article>
</div>
<p class="objects-tier">Handed back by a room</p>
<div class="objects-row">
<article class="object" data-tone="warm">
<p class="object-name">seat</p>
<p class="object-from"><code>subscribe</code>, with your <b>inbox</b>.</p>
<p class="object-calls"><code>post</code> <code>history</code> <code>typing</code> <code>away</code> <code>leave</code> <code>membershipKind</code> <code>mintInvitation</code></p>
<p class="object-back">Hands back a <b>line</b>, an <b>invitation</b>, or the lines the room kept.</p>
</article>
<article class="object" data-tone="warm">
<p class="object-name">files</p>
<p class="object-from">The fourth slot of <code>subscribe</code>'s answer, in a room that can hold files.</p>
<p class="object-calls"><code>attach</code> <code>postFiles</code></p>
<p class="object-back">Hands back an <b>upload</b> per file, and posts a line with its files.</p>
</article>
<article class="object" data-tone="warm">
<p class="object-name">subject</p>
<p class="object-from">The third slot of <code>subscribe</code>'s answer, for participants.</p>
<p class="object-calls"><code>setSubject</code></p>
<p class="object-back">Hands back nothing. The room tells everyone.</p>
</article>
</div>
<p class="objects-tier">Handed back by your seat and files</p>
<div class="objects-row">
<article class="object" data-tone="warm">
<p class="object-name">line</p>
<p class="object-from"><code>post</code>. The line you just said.</p>
<p class="object-calls"><code>edit</code> <code>withdraw</code> <code>refile</code></p>
<p class="object-back">Hands back nothing. The room tells everyone through their inbox.</p>
</article>
<article class="object" data-tone="warm">
<p class="object-name">upload and blob</p>
<p class="object-from"><code>attach</code> hands you one upload per file. The room hands everyone a blob for it.</p>
<p class="object-calls"><code>write</code> <code>discard</code> <code>read</code></p>
<p class="object-back">Bytes go in through the upload and come out of the blob, from an offset, up to a length.</p>
</article>
<article class="object" data-tone="warm">
<p class="object-name">invitation</p>
<p class="object-from"><code>mintInvitation</code> on a seat.</p>
<p class="object-calls"><code>claimInvitation</code></p>
<p class="object-back">Hands back the <b>room</b>, exactly once. Then <code>subscribe</code> for a seat.</p>
</article>
</div>
<p class="objects-tier">Yours</p>
<div class="objects-row">
<article class="object" data-tone="own">
<p class="object-name">inbox</p>
<p class="object-from">You make it, and hand it to rooms and to the directory. Rooms call <code>notify</code> on it. Other members call the rest.</p>
<p class="object-calls"><code>notify</code> <code>dm</code> <code>directReady</code> <code>handRoom</code> <code>offerRoom</code> <code>handArchive</code> <code>offerArchive</code> <code>offer</code> <code>candidate</code></p>
<p class="object-back">Receives every line a room sends you, whatever another member hands you directly, and the signaling offers and candidates a WebRTC exchange routes to you.</p>
</article>
</div>
</div>
```

No code checks who is calling. A member without the line reference has
nothing to send `edit` to. A reference can also be handed on. Give your
line's reference to someone and they can take the line down. Give the subject
reference to a bot and it can keep the subject, without the room learning what
a bot is.

A subject is a description, and it can also link an object the room is about,
such as a board or a game, by its `ocapn://` sturdyref. The room checks that
the link parses and tells it to everyone in the room, but never calls the
object. Whoever set it hosts the object and decides what that reference may
do. A subject with no link is a plain topic.

# What the room tells you

The room calls `notify` on your inbox. After the text, a line carries a tag
that says what kind of thing it is. A client that does not know a tag skips
it and draws the line anyway, so the room can say more over time without
every client being rebuilt first.

| Tag | Then | Meaning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `line` | id, then any file records | An ordinary line, under the id this room knows it by |
| `edited` | id | That line now reads as this text |
| `refiled` | id, then the new file records | That line's files are now these |
| `withdrawn` | id | That line is taken back |
| `roster` | names | Who is here, sent when you arrive |
| `join`, `leave` | name | Someone came or went |
| `typing` | name, on or off | Someone is typing, never stored |
| `away` | name, reason | Someone stepped away. An empty reason means they are back |
| `subject` | description, then an optional link | What the room is about. An empty description means taken down |
| `line-bytes` | a number | The longest line this room will carry |
| `listed`, `unlisted` | | Whether this room is on the public list, sent when you arrive |
| `membershipEnded` | | This room has released the seat behind your inbox |

# How long each thing lives

| What | Lives as long as | To get it again |
| --- | --- | --- |
| A room entrance reference | Your connection to the server | `enter` by name, or redeem the room's link |
| A seat | Until you `leave`, are kicked, or disconnect | `subscribe` again |
| A line reference | Your connection | It is not reissued. Hand it on if someone else should hold it |
| A room link | Until the manager releases it | It is a string. Keep it |
| An invitation | One use | `mintInvitation` again |
| A direct connection | Until either side closes it | Ask the directory to introduce you again |
| A steward | Until the room's moderator capability is rotated | Ask the manager again. It hands back the same one |
| An admin secret | Until the server restarts without a data directory | It is written into the data directory |

A live reference dies with its connection. To enter again later, Rooms asks
the server for a durable link, an OCapN sturdyref. The link keeps the right
to enter and the room's identity, not a live room or seat. Redeeming it gives
a fresh entrance reference, and the first member to join brings the room back
after a restart or after everyone left. A private room's link says nothing
about the room's label, and the label says nothing about the link.

# An introduction, call by call

A direct conversation is the introduction OCapN calls a three-party handoff.
Here it is as the calls that make it, with Mira asking to message the lead
bot:

| Step | Who calls | Call | What happens |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | Both, before introduction | `register` on the directory, then `offerInbox` | Each has a name and has offered the server an inbox for introductions |
| 2 | Mira | `introduce` on the directory, naming the bot | The server hands Mira a reference to the inbox the bot offered |
| 3 | Mira's peer | Redeems that reference with the bot's peer | A connection opens between the two peers. The server is not on it |
| 4 | Mira | `dm` on the bot's inbox, with her own reply inbox alongside | The first line arrives, and the bot's replies come back to that inbox. When the bot cannot dial Mira's side, it dials the other way and sends `directReady` to set up the reply route with no chat message |
| 5 | Either | `handRoom`, `offerRoom`, `handArchive`, `offerArchive` | References move between them over the same connection, as live handoffs or as durable links |

When the two peers are browsers, WebRTC's connection details travel through
the directory as `offerSignaling`, `signalOffer`, `signalAnswer`,
`signalCandidate`, and `signalDone`, until the two are talking and the server
drops out. A peer that will not answer an offer sends `signalDecline` with its
reason, and the offering peer hears it right away.

# Files, call by call

| Step | Call | On | What happens |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | `attach` | your **files** reference | One **upload** reference per file |
| 2 | `postFiles` | your **files** reference | The line is posted with its file records, and every member gets a **blob** per file at once |
| 3 | `write`, chunk by chunk | the upload | Bytes arrive at the room |
| 4 | `read` with offset and length | the blob | A member reads. A read that reaches bytes still on their way in waits for them |

In a direct conversation the sender hosts the file on the direct connection
and the other side reads it the same way. The room never sees those bytes.

# What OCapN gives Rooms

- **No ambient authority.** There is no account table and no permission
  check by name anywhere in the tables above. If you can form the call, you
  may make it, because forming it means holding the reference.
- **Introductions across three parties.** The server can hand you a
  reference to something another peer hosts, and your peer redeems it with
  them directly. Direct messages, invitations, and shared archives are all
  this one move.
- **Promises.** A call answers a promise, so a client can keep sending to a
  room while its `open` is still on the way back.
- **Durable links.** A sturdyref names something a host can bring back across
  restarts and connections. Room links, invitations, and admin secrets are all
  sturdyrefs.

Clients in Zig, Rust, Python, Racket, TypeScript, and the browser all speak
this protocol. So does a [Spritely Goblins](https://spritely.institute/)
program: it can sit in a room and answer a direct message without any change
to Goblins, because Rooms uses only OCapN.

The readable specification, with the reasoning behind every verb, is
[`protocol.ts`](https://github.com/glowcap-org/glowcap/blob/main/apps/rooms/lib/protocol.ts)
for the directory, rooms, seats, lines, files, and introductions. Other files
cover
[handover](https://github.com/glowcap-org/glowcap/blob/main/apps/rooms/lib/protocol_handover.ts),
[IRC](https://github.com/glowcap-org/glowcap/blob/main/apps/rooms/lib/protocol_irc.ts),
and the
[admin objects](https://github.com/glowcap-org/glowcap/blob/main/apps/rooms/lib/protocol_operator.ts).
`zig build gen-rooms-protocol` generates the core verbs into each client.
[How Rooms works]($link.page('rooms/architecture')) shows how the code that
speaks it is divided.
